To handle the 300+ spell matrix, you can set up a hybrid casting system that offers flexibility depending on player builds, equipment, and combat situations.

Spell Casting Outlets & System Mechanics

Catalyst Casting (Staffs, Wands, & Grimoires):

Primary Function: Acts as a channel for pure ranged magic, elemental AoE spells, and complex spell matrices (like high-tier Void, Gravity, or Light magic).

Mechanical Advantage: Equipping a staff or grimoire lowers MP cost, reduces spell cast/channelling times, and increases spell range/radius.

Body & Bare-Handed Casting (Inner Ki & Aura):

Primary Function: Utilizes the caster's body as the conduit for instant-cast spells, defensive barriers, self-buffs, mobility bursts, and point-blank offensive blasts (e.g., firing a Fireball straight from the palm or surging Lava through a kick).

Mechanical Advantage: Zero equipment requirement and faster initial frame startup, but slightly higher stamina/MP consumption and reduced projectile range compared to staff casting.

Spell-Blade Conduit System (Hybrid Channeling):

Primary Function: Allows the player to infuse active spells directly into equipped swords using the spell matrix.

Mechanical Advantage: Instead of firing a standalone Void/Gravity spell from the hand or a staff, the player can coat their sword with the spell effect, enabling those 300+ spells to temporarily enhance melee slash arcs and weapon strikes.

This structure allows staff users to act as long-range sorcerers, body/ki casters to act as high-mobility brawlers, and sword wielders to blend the spell matrix straight into their slash attacks.

